bl:StoryAgentOrchestrationRedesign a backlog:Story, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    backlog:belongsToLineage bl:CodeAbundanceLineage ;
    backlog:hasIdentifier "STORY-10" ;
    backlog:hasState backlog:Proposed ;
    backlog:memberOfContainer bl:CodeAbundanceBacklog ;
    backlog:effectiveDefinitionOfDone bl:DoDCodeAbundance ;
    backlog:dependsOn bl:StoryShortFieldRewrite ;
    backlog:hasInvestmentCategory backlog:Cat_TechnicalDebt ;
    backlog:notYetScoreable true ;
    backlog:hasScoreabilityReason "Freshly identified by direct investigation of the real orchestration code (2026-09-02); not yet run through the framework's prioritisation method." ;
    rdfs:label "Map the real dependency graph among the 11 pipeline agents; parallelize what genuinely has no dependency; extend failure-reason-and-retry to all 11 stages"@en ;
    backlog:asRole "the package maintainer" ;
    backlog:wantsCapability "the 11-agent pipeline (Intent, Story, Grooming, Product Owner, Lineage Compose, Conformance, Execution, Sprint Planning, DevOps, Code Gen, Test Gen) to run stages with no real data dependency between them concurrently rather than always sequentially, coordinated by one or more explicit coordinator functions (one per dependency tier, matching the real graph -- not one monolithic coordinator forcing every stage through a single choke point) that dispatch each tier's independent stages together and gate the next tier on their real completion, and every stage, not only Code Gen/Test Gen, to report a real failure reason back to its own coordinator and get one corrective retry" ;
    backlog:soThat "direct user finding, checked against the real code before agreeing or disagreeing: 'agents should work in parallel not sequential.' Confirmed by reading runRequestIntakeAgent directly: the pipeline is genuinely sequential today (each stage awaited before the next begins), and at least some of that sequencing is real, not arbitrary -- a disclosed code comment (DEF-25) shows Grooming and Lineage Compose both consume Intent's own already-repaired acceptance criterion as real input, a genuine data dependency, not just convention. Separately confirmed: withOneAutoFix already implements a real failure-reason-then-retry pattern (the exact shape the user asked for), but only for 2 of the 11 stages (Code Gen, Test Gen) -- the other 9 have no such mechanism. This Story's real first task is mapping which of the 11 stages' inputs/outputs actually depend on each other (only genuine independence can be safely parallelized) before touching any execution code -- not assumed achievable by a quick Promise.all wrap, which would risk breaking real data flow between stages. COMPLETE DEPENDENCY MAP, built by reading every call site (2026-09-02): Tier 0 (root): Intent, input = raw request only. Tier 1 (all depend on Intent ONLY, confirmed genuinely parallelizable with each other): Story Author (intent.title, intent.ac), Product Owner (intent.title only), Code Gen (intent.ac only), Test Gen (intent.ac only, independent of Code Gen's own output too). Tier 2: Grooming (needs Story Author's asRole/wantsCapability/soThat), Lineage Compose (needs Story Author's authored object), Execution (needs Code Gen's codeRes AND Test Gen's testRes together). Tier 3: Conformance (needs Lineage Compose's draftTurtle), Sprint Planner (needs Grooming's groom.ready AND Lineage Compose's slug). Tier 4: DevOps (needs Execution's ran/ok AND Code Gen's codeRes AND Test Gen's testSafe). NOT implemented this pass: the function is tightly coupled to progressive UI rendering (begin/finish/paint, an accordion whose visual order depends on call order) and cannot be behaviourally tested in this environment -- running the real pipeline requires WEBLLM_STATE==='ready', a live LLM connection unavailable here. Shipping an unverifiable rewrite of live async orchestration was judged worse than leaving it correct and sequential; implementation needs an environment where the rewrite can actually be exercised end-to-end before shipping." .

